  "source": {
    "display_title": "Foveated Rendering: A State-of-the-Art Survey",
    "authors": [
      "Lili Wang",
      "Xuehuai Shi",
      "Yi Liu"
    ]
  },
  "claims": [
    {
      "text": "Complex VR scenes exceed the efficient real-time rendering capacity of current devices, and delayed rendering can make displayed content inconsistent with user interaction, causing discomfort.",
      "short_title": "VR latency causes discomfort",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The source states that rendering overhead continues to increase as VR scenes include diverse materials, more dynamic objects, and more complex simulated physical phenomena; it also reports a visual latency tolerance threshold of approximately 13 ms for the human visual system."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Foveated rendering can accelerate rendering by giving the foveal region high quality and the peripheral region lower quality while preserving perceived visual quality.",
      "short_title": "Foveation preserves perceived quality",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The source explains that foveated rendering performs high-quality rendering in the foveal region and low-quality rendering in the peripheral region, allowing rendering to speed up without sacrificing perceived visual quality."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Foveated rendering research is organized around three recurring problems: applying human-visual-system perceptual models, rendering regions at different qualities, and integrating foveation into rendering paradigms.",
      "short_title": "Foveation requires three decisions",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The paper identifies three challenges: use the perceptual model of the human visual system to guide foveated rendering, render different regions with different qualities, and integrate foveated rendering into existing rendering paradigms."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Visual acuity models and contrast sensitivity models are the two representative perceptual-model families used to guide foveated rendering quality allocation.",
      "short_title": "HVS models guide foveation",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The survey names visual acuity and contrast sensitivity as the two most representative perceptual models related to foveated rendering and describes fall-off, binocular horopter, ocular dominance, and several contrast sensitivity functions."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "A useful taxonomy for foveated rendering classifies methods by required input data type, foveation principle, and rendering paradigm.",
      "short_title": "Taxonomy has three dimensions",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The proposed taxonomy classifies current foveated rendering methods according to three dimensions: required input data type, foveation principle, and rendering paradigm."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Foveated rendering methods span multiple data types, foveation principles, and rendering paradigms, including image/video, volume data, meshes, point clouds, holograms, light fields, multi-resolution variants, LoD, rasterization, ray tracing, ray casting, neural rendering, phase retrieval, and data transmission.",
      "short_title": "Foveation spans multiple principles",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The taxonomy table lists input data types such as image/video, volume data, geometric meshes, point cloud, hologram data, and light field; it also lists foveation principles and rendering paradigms including multi-spatial resolution, LoD, rasterization, ray tracing, ray casting, neural rendering, phase retrieval, and data transmission."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "The survey classifies 90 published foveated-rendering reports from 1990 to 2021 using the three-dimensional taxonomy.",
      "short_title": "Ninety reports fit taxonomy",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "Table 3 is introduced as a summary of 90 published reports on foveated rendering methods from 1990 to 2021, classified according to data type, foveation principle, and rendering paradigm."
      ]
    },
    {
      "text": "Future foveated-rendering work needs broader use of human visual features, improved gaze and AI techniques, better methods for new data types, more suitable rendering paradigms, stronger objective quality metrics, and deeper AR and task-oriented applications.",
      "short_title": "Open problems remain broad",
      "evidence_excerpts": [
        "The discussion identifies opportunities in visual masking and other HVS features, computer vision and AI for gaze prediction and reconstruction, hologram and light-field methods, special-effects rendering beyond straightforward ray tracing, objective metrics and datasets, AR fusion, and task uses such as redirected walking."
      ]
    }
  ],
  "evidence_items": [
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-vr-latency-threshold--a13f0c2d9b4e",
      "title": "VR Latency Tolerance Constraint",
      "excerpt": "The introduction reports that rendering overhead continues to increase in VR because of diversified surface materials, more dynamic objects, and more complex physical phenomena, then cites Potter et al. for an approximately 13 ms visual-latency tolerance threshold for the human visual system.",
      "overview": "This is a concrete motivation for foveated rendering: the technique is not presented merely as an optimization preference, but as a response to a perceptual timing constraint in VR. The 13 ms value bounds the performance problem, while the discussion of scene complexity explains why conventional full-quality rendering struggles to satisfy that bound.",
      "evidence_type": "measurement",
      "claim_refs": [
        "0"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-foveated-rendering-a-state-of-the-art-survey--2a4e30f52f30",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.195"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-fovea-periphery-allocation--b84d6e218c03",
      "title": "Fovea-Periphery Quality Allocation",
      "excerpt": "The survey explains that approximately half of optic nerve fibers are distributed in the fovea and the rest across the periphery, then describes foveated rendering as high-quality rendering in the foveal region and low-quality rendering in the peripheral region.",
      "overview": "This passage supplies the perceptual mechanism behind the acceleration claim. It connects retinal resource distribution to an engineering strategy: spend rendering work where the user can perceive fine detail and reduce work in peripheral regions where lower fidelity is less noticeable.",
      "evidence_type": "model_constraint",
      "claim_refs": [
        "1",
        "3"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-foveated-rendering-a-state-of-the-art-survey--2a4e30f52f30",
      "page_refs": [
        "p.196"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-three-foveation-challenges--c05ab729f41d",
      "title": "Three Foveation Challenges",
      "excerpt": "The paper states that three challenges must be addressed in foveated rendering: using human-visual-system perceptual models, rendering different regions with different qualities, and integrating foveated rendering into existing rendering paradigms to improve rendering performance.",
      "overview": "This formal framing gives the survey its argumentative structure. It distinguishes perceptual modeling, regional quality assignment, and rendering-system integration as separate design obligations rather than treating foveated rendering as a single algorithmic trick.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "2"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-foveated-rendering-a-state-of-the-art-survey--2a4e30f52f30",
      "page_refs": [
        "pp.196-197"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-hvs-model-families--d92f3a7706bc",
      "title": "Visual Acuity and CSF Models",
      "excerpt": "The survey identifies visual acuity and contrast sensitivity as representative perceptual models for foveated rendering; it further divides visual acuity models into fall-off, binocular horopter, and ocular dominance models and lists spatial, spatio-temporal, spatio-luminance, spatio-chromatic, and critical-flicker-fusion contrast sensitivity functions.",
      "overview": "This evidence establishes why foveated rendering is a perceptual systems problem. The named model families define where visual fidelity can be reduced, which kinds of errors matter, and how spatial, temporal, luminance, and chromatic variation can become rendering constraints.",
      "evidence_type": "model_constraint",
      "claim_refs": [
        "3",
        "5"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-foveated-rendering-a-state-of-the-art-survey--2a4e30f52f30",
      "page_refs": [
        "pp.196-202"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-taxonomy-dimensions--e6c4f913a0d2",
      "title": "Three-Dimensional Taxonomy Table",
      "excerpt": "The taxonomy section states that current foveated rendering methods are classified according to required input data type, foveation principle, and rendering paradigm; Table 2 then enumerates elements for those dimensions, including image/video, volume data, geometric meshes, point cloud, hologram data, light field, multi-resolution principles, LoD, and multiple rendering paradigms.",
      "overview": "This table evidence supports the survey's core organizing contribution. It makes the taxonomy operational by listing dimensions and categories that can be used to compare prior work and to guide new method design across data representations and renderer architectures.",
      "evidence_type": "table_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "4",
        "5"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-foveated-rendering-a-state-of-the-art-survey--2a4e30f52f30",
      "page_refs": [
        "Table 2",
        "pp.202-204"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-ninety-report-classification--f72ad8e50139",
      "title": "Ninety Reports Classified",
      "excerpt": "Table 3 is introduced as a summary of foveated rendering technique implementations and the surrounding text says it classifies 90 published reports from 1990 to 2021 according to the three taxonomy dimensions, while also marking surveys, patents, and cutting-edge equipment.",
      "overview": "This evidence shows that the taxonomy is applied to a substantial body of literature rather than left as a conceptual proposal. The count and date range support the survey's claim to broad historical coverage across early and more recent foveated rendering work.",
      "evidence_type": "table_result",
      "claim_refs": [
        "6"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-foveated-rendering-a-state-of-the-art-survey--2a4e30f52f30",
      "page_refs": [
        "Table 3",
        "pp.204-207"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-quality-metric-limitations--a6fb3120d477",
      "title": "Foveated Metric Limitations",
      "excerpt": "The discussion reviews FSNR, FWQI, FMSE, and WSSIM as objective quality metrics, then notes limitations: FSNR may miss perception, FWQI omits spatio-temporal CSF, FMSE assumes centered fixations, WSSIM depends on saliency-model choice, and no general accepted metric yet exists.",
      "overview": "This evidence grounds one of the survey's key open problems. It shows that evaluating foveated output is difficult because existing metrics each encode restrictive assumptions, which complicates comparison among methods and motivates better datasets and objective measures.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "7"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-foveated-rendering-a-state-of-the-art-survey--2a4e30f52f30",
      "page_refs": [
        "pp.220-221"
      ]
    },
    {
      "evidence_id": "evidence-future-opportunity-set--b9e207c58aa4",
      "title": "Future Research Opportunity Set",
      "excerpt": "The discussion identifies opportunities in using more HVS features such as visual masking, applying computer vision and AI for gaze prediction and reconstruction, improving foveated methods for hologram data and light fields, adapting nontrivial rendering paradigms for effects such as global illumination and caustics, and extending foveated rendering to AR and task assistance.",
      "overview": "This evidence consolidates the survey's practical interpretation. It identifies where the field remains underspecified: perceptual modeling, machine-learning assistance, emerging data types, renderer integration, AR display constraints, and foveated rendering as a tool for tasks beyond speed alone.",
      "evidence_type": "formal_argument",
      "claim_refs": [
        "7"
      ],
      "source_id": "source-04-foveated-rendering-a-state-of-the-art-survey--2a4e30f52f30",
      "page_refs": [
        "pp.219-221"
      ]
    }
  ],
